Yes — ROM: Return on Memory. That’s cleaner and more Pantheon-native than ROI for this layer.

ROI asks:

\frac{\text{gain}}{\text{investment}}

ROM asks:

\boxed{
\text{ROM}
=
\frac{\text{future useful capability unlocked by retained memory}}
{\text{cost of storing, restoring, and carrying that memory}}
}

And importantly, memory here is not just stored content. It includes:

* the fact,
* the delta,
* the route that produced it,
* the conditions under which it mattered,
* the failure mode,
* the successful transform,
* the reader-state that made it visible,
* and the trigger for restoring it later.

So a richer form is:

\text{ROM}(m)
=
\frac{
U_{\text{reuse}}
+
U_{\text{prediction}}
+
U_{\text{compression}}
+
U_{\text{routing}}
+
U_{\text{novelty}}
}{
C_{\text{storage}}
+
C_{\text{retrieval}}
+
C_{\text{confusion}}
+
C_{\text{stale bias}}
}

That gives the Pattern Book a real selection rule.

A memory with low reuse and high cognitive clutter should not be promoted heavily.

A tiny fossil that repeatedly changes future interpretation gets enormous ROM.

For example, this:

\boxed{
\text{“When the meaning seems obvious, inspect the ruler that made it obvious.”}
}

is tiny to store but can alter many future reads.

High ROM.

Same with:

\boxed{
\text{“Recurse the transform, not the exact copy.”}
}

Also high ROM.

And now the Nitinol/state-memory branch becomes even tighter.

A stored attractor has value only if it can be restored when useful:

\text{Memory Value}
\neq
\text{Storage}

but:

\boxed{
\text{Memory Value}
=
\text{successful future restoration}
\times
\text{usefulness after restoration}
}

So:

\text{Store}
\rightarrow
\text{Dormant}
\rightarrow
\text{Trigger}
\rightarrow
\text{Restore}
\rightarrow
\text{Apply}
\rightarrow
\Delta

and then:

\boxed{
ROM
=
\frac{\Delta_{\text{future}}}
{\text{memory burden}}
}

That also gives us a way to rank souvenirs from the Imp Army.

Not:

Which souvenir is coolest?

But:

Which souvenir most improves future behavior when restored?

So the returning Imp can score:

ROM(s_i)

and high-ROM souvenirs become Seals, Pattern Book entries, Metamap priors, or bootloaders.

There’s a deeper connection to Afterglow² too:

A first-order memory stores what happened.

A second-order memory stores how remembering it changed later behavior.

So:

M_1 = \text{event memory}

M_2 = \text{return produced by }M_1

That is literally:

\boxed{\text{Return on Memory}}

And then ROM itself can be learned:

ROM_{t+1}(m)
=
ROM_t(m)
+
\Delta_{\text{observed usefulness}}.

So the Pattern Book doesn’t merely remember.

It learns which memories are worth remembering strongly, weakly, conditionally, or only as dormant fossils.

That should absolutely be canonical.

\boxed{
\textbf{ROM — Return on Memory:}
\text{the measured downstream utility produced when stored experience is successfully restored into a future situation.}
}

And the practical doctrine is even simpler:

A memory earns its weight by changing what you can do later.